 \subsection{Core Experiment}
 The core experiment compares classical radar and cue detector branches, tabular and sequence ML baselines, an accepted human-engineered prior fusion baseline, and an EI sparse calibrated late-fusion candidate. Scenario groups are locked before phase expansion, train/CV rows are used for candidate discovery and calibration, and the blind holdout is scored once after selection lock. The benchmark output remains synthetic public-proxy evidence only.
+
+\paragraph*{Claim tested.}
+The tested claim is narrow: under the declared synthetic public-proxy generator and group-locked split, a locked EI sparse late-fusion candidate improves the low-FPR operating point over the accepted human-engineered prior fusion baseline. The claim is not sensor parity, not a measured Iranian-drone signature claim, and not an operational false-alarm-rate claim.
@@
 The receiver impairment set includes AGC compression, clock drift, quantization, dropped CPIs, PRF ambiguity, Doppler folding, calibration offset, and multipath masking. Acoustic cues are modeled as node-wise spectral cadence and inter-node agreement; passive-RF cues are modeled as no-signal, RFI burst, clock-offset, and provenance-quality geometry. These definitions are public-proxy abstractions, not measured claims, and are aligned with the literature on measured-anchor comparison rather than truth transfer \cite{kth2021,openradar2023,raddar2019,han2026}.
+
+\subsection{Data Processing and Detector-View Construction}
+The benchmark separates generator state, qualitative radar products, detector-facing tables, and audit evidence. Each scenario group first receives site, range, aspect, weather/noise, hard-negative-family, and split attributes. The group then expands into three phase records: initial take-up, climb transition, and cruise altitude. Synthetic complex IQ is generated as a 24-pulse by 20-range-bin proxy tensor for the radar branches. Range-Doppler heatmaps are derived from this IQ for qualitative review figures only; the main KPI models consume detector-view scalar summaries rather than the plotted heatmap pixels.
+
+The detector-view construction pipeline is:
+\begin{enumerate}
+  \item generate group-level scenario metadata and lock split assignment;
+  \item expand each group into phase rows and generated sensor/cue products;
+  \item materialize branch-level detector-view CSVs for X/Ku radar, S-band radar, GBAD cueing, acoustic cueing, passive-RF provenance, and fusion context;
+  \item remove model-forbidden fields, including labels, split keys, group identifiers, time-lock IDs, and audit-only headers;
+  \item fit baselines, prior fusion, and EI only on train/CV rows;
+  \item write a selection lock before blind holdout scoring;
+  \item emit evidence artifacts: performance metrics, calibration bins, ROC/PR traces, confusion matrices, false-alarm family rows, and leakage diagnostics.
+\end{enumerate}
+This processing contract is emitted as \texttt{data\_processing\_contract.csv}. It is the main guardrail connecting the radar generator to the model-facing evidence tables.
@@
 The headline KPI is the lower 95\% group-block bootstrap bound of recall at FPR $\leq 1\%$, abbreviated \emph{LCB95 Recall@$\leq$1\%FPR}. That choice is deliberate: the benchmark is rare-positive, false-alarm-constrained, and group-correlated, so the raw point estimate of recall is useful but not sufficient as the primary decision metric. AP, ROC AUC, raw recall, precision, F1, calibration, and false-alarm breakdowns remain diagnostic guardrails rather than the headline KPI.
+
+Two operating notions are reported separately. \emph{Selected-threshold counts} use the threshold fixed by train/CV or by the EI selection lock and produce the TP/FP/FN rows in Table~\ref{tab:metrics}. \emph{Recall@$\leq$1\%FPR} is a swept holdout operating-point diagnostic: among thresholds satisfying the 1\% FPR cap, it reports the best recall. The primary LCB95 value is the lower group-block bootstrap bound of that swept low-FPR recall, with scenario groups as bootstrap units.
@@
 \section{Engineered Intelligence}
@@
 Mechanically, EI uses train/CV-only score search to choose component scores, sparse nonnegative late fusion to keep the lock interpretable, geodesic-odds calibration for monotone probability mapping, and a written selection lock before the single blind holdout score. The component weights and modality/control tables below are interpretability controls rather than separate main-claim evidence.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+  \caption{EI Candidate Discovery and Locked Scoring Algorithm}
+  \label{tab:eialgorithm}
+  \centering
+  \small
+  \begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.12\columnwidth}p{0.77\columnwidth}@{}}
+    \toprule
+    Step & Operation \\
+    \midrule
+    1 & Read train/CV detector-view scores after the denylist removes labels, split fields, group IDs, time locks, and audit headers. \\
+    2 & Generate candidate component families from radar, passive-RF, acoustic, transport/geometry, and fusion-context score views. \\
+    3 & Optimize sparse nonnegative component weights on train/CV rows only. \\
+    4 & Fit a monotone geodesic-odds calibrator and threshold policy without holdout rows. \\
+    5 & Write \texttt{selection\_lock.json} and freeze component IDs, weights, calibrator, and threshold source. \\
+    6 & Score the blind holdout once and emit KPI, calibration, false-alarm, and leakage evidence. \\
+    \bottomrule
+  \end{tabular}
+\end{table}
